// std/syswatch.jtr — "has anything under this directory changed?"
//
// TIER: `sys`. A watcher is a POLLABLE over a platform notification handle, which is what
// makes it the natural consumer of `runtime.Poller`: the loop already knows how to wait on
// something and run a task when it fires, and this module supplies one more thing to wait
// on.
//
// ─── What is here ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// One directory, NON-RECURSIVELY, per `Watcher`. A handle to hand `runtime.watch`, the
// `Poller` that can wait on it, and a `drain` that reports what changed. No recursive
// watching, no rename correlation, no debouncing — each of those is a policy, and a policy
// built on top of a primitive that reports two different amounts of information on two
// platforms would be a policy that means something different on each.
//
// ─── The one thing to understand before using this ────────────────────────────
//
// **A change notification is a HINT, not a log.** Every caller needs a path that rescans
// and works out the truth for itself, and this module's job is to make that rescan RARE,
// not to make it unnecessary. That is not a Windows concession — it is true on both
// platforms, for two different reasons:
//
//   * Windows `FindFirstChangeNotification` reports THAT something under the directory
//     changed and never says what. There is no name and no kind.
//   * Linux `inotify` names the file and the kind — until its per-watch queue overflows,
//     at which point the kernel drops events and sets `IN_Q_OVERFLOW`. A watcher that did
//     not surface that would silently miss changes, which is the worst failure a build tool
//     can have.
//
// Both arrive as `WATCH_RESCAN` / `WATCH_OVERFLOW`, so a caller writes ONE recovery path
// and it is exercised on Linux too rather than being Windows-only code that nobody runs.
// `names_changes()` says which world you are in, so a caller can skip work it knows is
// impossible instead of discovering it at runtime.
//
// ─── The POSIX branch is LINUX, and the vocabulary cannot say so ──────────────
//
// `inotify` is a Linux interface. POSIX never standardised file watching, so there is no
// portable spelling: macOS has `kqueue`/`FSEvents`, which is a different model, not a
// different name. `<sys/inotify.h>` does not exist on macOS, so this module does not
// compile there — a MISSING HEADER at the line that names it, which is the loudest and
// earliest failure available, rather than a link error or a branch that quietly does
// nothing.
//
// `std/sysdir`'s `D_NAME_OFFSET` comment already records that `linux`/`macos` become worth
// adding to the `@cfg` vocabulary "when something needs to tell them apart". **This is the
// second thing that needs it, and it is a sharper case than the first** — `sysdir` wanted
// to tell them apart to pick a NUMBER, this one needs to pick an entirely different API.
// They are still not added here, and the reason is now more specific than "nobody can run
// it": `posix` is a SUPERSET of `linux` and `macos`, and `src/attrs.rs`'s vocabulary is a
// closed list of guards that are disjoint by construction. Adding two nested names would
// make `@cfg(posix) fn f` and `@cfg(linux) fn f` both emit on Linux — a duplicate
// definition — so the increment is not a word in a list, it is a specificity rule about
// which of two overlapping items wins. Recorded rather than half-done.
//
// ─── `read(2)` is unbindable, and `readv(2)` is the way through ───────────────
//
// Draining an inotify fd needs `read(2)`, and **`read` is a Jestyr KEYWORD** (the `read x:
// T` parameter mode), so `extern "unistd.h" fn read` does not parse. That is §2.1b's rule
// arriving from the other side: an extern's name belongs to the C symbol namespace, and
// that namespace contains words Jestyr has spent on its own grammar.
//
// `readv` is the same call with a vector, and binding it costs one foreign structure:
//
//     struct iovec { void *iov_base; size_t iov_len; };
//
// **It is built out of `usize`, not out of bytes at offsets**, which is the whole reason it
// is acceptable where `std/sysfs` refuses `struct stat`. `iovec` is two pointer-sized words
// in a specified order with no room for padding, so an `alloc(usize, 2)` has its layout by
// construction — there is no offset to get wrong and no endianness to assume. The single
// assumption left is `sizeof(size_t) == sizeof(void *)`, and
// `a_pointer_and_a_usize_are_the_same_width` asserts it on EVERY host, Windows included,
// with `@size_of` — so the check runs on the platform that never compiles this branch.
//
// ─── The Windows limitation this module cannot fix, stated plainly ────────────
//
// A `Runtime` holds exactly ONE `Poller`. On Linux that is enough for everything, because a
// socket and an inotify fd are both file descriptors and one `poll(2)` waits on both — so
// `syspoll.host()` services a watcher with no new code at all, and `poller()` simply hands
// it back.
//
// On Windows they are not the same kind of object. A change-notification HANDLE is waited
// on with `WaitForSingleObject`; `WSAPoll` takes sockets and nothing else. **So a Windows
// runtime can serve sockets or watches, not both**, and which one it serves is decided by
// which `Poller` was installed. The fix is not a wrapper: it is `WSAEventSelect`, which
// brings a socket into the object-wait world at the cost of making it non-blocking — a
// change to `std/sysnet`'s semantics, not an addition to this module. Recorded in
// `docs/session-notes/jestyr-std-v4-file-watching-handoff.md` §2.3.
//
// What the Windows poller does keep, and what `a_real_change_wakes_the_loop_and_drains`
// exercises: it waits on its own handle for the WHOLE timeout even when that handle is not
// among the ones it was asked about, so a runtime holding a foreign handle idles correctly
// and merely never fires, rather than spinning. Never falsely ready, never spinning — the
// two properties a poller that cannot see everything can still keep.
//
// ─── What this does NOT prove ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Not that you have seen every change: see the rescan discussion above. Not that a file is
// finished being written — Linux `IN_CLOSE_WRITE` says a writer closed it, but another
// writer may already have it open, and Windows says nothing of the kind. And not the ORDER
// of changes across a rename: `IN_MOVED_FROM`/`IN_MOVED_TO` carry a cookie that correlates
// the two halves, which this module deliberately does not expose, because Windows cannot
// answer the question at all and an API that could only be used on one platform would be a
// worse lie than not having it.
//
// Worked example:  examples/std/syswatch_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/syswatch_test.jtr

import "syserr"
import "cstring"
import "syspoll"
import "runtime"
import "fs"

// ---- POSIX (Linux) bindings ---------------------------------------------------
//
// Header-declared, so no prototype is emitted and the platform's own declaration applies.
// `inotify_init1`'s fd and `close`'s are `int`; they are bound as `i64` for the same reason
// `std/sysnet` unifies its socket signatures — C converts implicitly at the call, and one
// width across the module means no truncation to reason about.
//
// This `close` is libc's. `std/file` and `std/sysdir` each declare a Jestyr `close`, so the
// canonicaliser has already renamed both and the bare name is free — see `runtime.fire_due`
// for the case where it was not. **This module's own closing operation is therefore called
// `close_watch`**, because a Jestyr `close` HERE would collide with the extern in its own
// module, which is the one arrangement neither the canonicaliser nor the loader can fix.

@cfg(posix) extern "sys/inotify.h" fn inotify_init1(flags: i32) -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "sys/inotify.h" fn inotify_add_watch(fd: i64, path: cstr, mask: u32) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "sys/inotify.h" fn inotify_rm_watch(fd: i64, wd: i32) -> i32
@cfg(posix) extern "sys/uio.h" fn readv(fd: i64, iov: cptr, n: i32) -> i64
@cfg(posix) extern "unistd.h" fn close(fd: i64) -> i32

// ---- Windows bindings ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// `FindFirstChangeNotificationA` returns a HANDLE that goes SIGNALED when something under
// the directory changes and STAYS signaled until `FindNextChangeNotification` re-arms it.
// That is level-triggered by construction, which is exactly what `runtime.watch` promises
// its callers, so the two agree without either one adapting.

@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn FindFirstChangeNotificationA(path: cstr, subtree: i32, filter: u32) -> cptr
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn FindNextChangeNotification(h: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn FindCloseChangeNotification(h: cptr) -> i32
@cfg(windows) extern "windows.h" fn WaitForSingleObject(h: cptr, ms: u32) -> i32

// ---- what a change can be -----------------------------------------------------
//
// Six kinds, and the last two are the ones that make the API honest. `WATCH_RESCAN` and
// `WATCH_OVERFLOW` both mean "my report is incomplete, go and look" — they are kept
// separate because they have different causes and a caller may want to count them
// differently: a `RESCAN` every time is normal on Windows, and an `OVERFLOW` on Linux means
// the loop is not draining fast enough.

pub const WATCH_CREATED: i32 = 1
pub const WATCH_DELETED: i32 = 2
pub const WATCH_WRITTEN: i32 = 3     // a writer closed the file
pub const WATCH_MOVED_OUT: i32 = 4
pub const WATCH_MOVED_IN: i32 = 5
pub const WATCH_RESCAN: i32 = 6      // something changed; this platform cannot say what
pub const WATCH_OVERFLOW: i32 = 7    // events were LOST; the caller must rescan

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn kind_name(kind: i32) -> str {
    if kind == WATCH_CREATED { return "created" }
    if kind == WATCH_DELETED { return "deleted" }
    if kind == WATCH_WRITTEN { return "written" }
    if kind == WATCH_MOVED_OUT { return "moved-out" }
    if kind == WATCH_MOVED_IN { return "moved-in" }
    if kind == WATCH_RESCAN { return "rescan" }
    return "overflow"
}

// True when this platform reports WHICH entry changed. A caller uses it to decide whether
// a name-keyed cache is worth keeping at all, rather than discovering at runtime that every
// name is empty.
@cfg(posix) @no_alloc @no_os pub fn names_changes() -> bool { return true }
@cfg(windows) @no_alloc @no_os pub fn names_changes() -> bool { return false }

// One change. **`@copy`: three scalars and no storage** — the NAME lives in the caller's
// byte buffer, at `[name_off .. name_off + name_len]`, so a batch of changes costs one
// allocation the caller already made and none here. `name_len` is 0 whenever the platform
// did not supply a name, which is always on Windows and is also true on Linux for the
// overflow marker.
@copy pub struct Change {
    pub kind: i32,
    pub name_off: usize,
    pub name_len: usize,
}

// The name of a change, as a view into the buffer `drain` filled. Empty when there is none.
//
// A free function rather than a method because the name and the change live apart on
// purpose: a `Change` that owned its name would be an allocation per event, which is the
// cost this shape exists to avoid.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn change_name(read c: Change, read names: []u8) -> str {
    if c.name_len == 0 { return "" }
    if c.name_off + c.name_len > names.len { return "" }
    return from_utf8(names[c.name_off .. c.name_off + c.name_len])
}

// ---- constants ----------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The inotify mask this module asks for. **`IN_MODIFY` is deliberately ABSENT and
// `IN_CLOSE_WRITE` is present instead**, and the difference is the single most common bug
// in a first file watcher: `IN_MODIFY` fires on every `write(2)`, so a build triggered by it
// runs against a file the compiler is still half way through writing. `IN_CLOSE_WRITE` fires
// once, when a writer closes. It is not a guarantee that nobody else still has the file open
// — see the header — but it is the difference between "usually right" and "reliably wrong".
//
//   IN_CLOSE_WRITE 0x0008    IN_MOVED_FROM 0x0040    IN_MOVED_TO 0x0080
//   IN_CREATE      0x0100    IN_DELETE     0x0200
const WATCH_IN_CLOSE_WRITE: u32 = 8
const WATCH_IN_MOVED_FROM: u32 = 64
const WATCH_IN_MOVED_TO: u32 = 128
const WATCH_IN_CREATE: u32 = 256
const WATCH_IN_DELETE: u32 = 512
const WATCH_IN_MASK: u32 = 968              // the five above, OR-ed
const WATCH_IN_Q_OVERFLOW: u32 = 16384

// `IN_NONBLOCK`, which is `O_NONBLOCK`, which is 0o4000 on every architecture that runs
// Linux and inotify together. It is hard-coded, and that is safe for a reason that does NOT
// apply to `std/sysfs`'s refused `st_mode` offset: a wrong flag here makes `inotify_init1`
// return `EINVAL` and the open FAIL, where a wrong struct offset reads a plausible number
// and reports a plausible lie. Loud beats silent, so the constant is allowed to be a
// constant.
//
// **Non-blocking, and the reason is platform AGREEMENT rather than convenience.** The
// readiness-first design `std/sysnet` argues for would have this fd blocking, drained only
// after the poller says ready. But Windows' `drain` cannot block — re-arming a notification
// handle is a non-blocking operation with no other form — so a blocking POSIX branch would
// give the two platforms `drain`s with different contracts. Non-blocking makes both mean
// "report what is here now, and nothing if nothing is", which is one contract to document
// and one to test. Inotify makes this free: a read returns whole events or none, so there is
// no short-read case to handle.
const WATCH_IN_NONBLOCK: i32 = 2048

// The kernel's read buffer. inotify REFUSES a read smaller than one event
// (`sizeof(struct inotify_event) + NAME_MAX + 1` = 16 + 255 + 1 = 272), so this is a floor
// and not a tuning knob; 8 KiB holds a healthy batch and keeps `drain` to one syscall in the
// common case.
const WATCH_BUF_CAP: usize = 8192

// `struct inotify_event`'s fixed header: `int wd; uint32_t mask; uint32_t cookie; uint32_t
// len;` then `len` bytes of NUL-padded name.
//
// **This IS a foreign structure read by offset, and it is the `sockaddr_in` case rather than
// the `struct stat` case.** It is a kernel WIRE format — the bytes a read of the fd hands
// back — fixed by the inotify ABI and identical on every Linux architecture, which is what
// `std/sysfs`'s header names as the distinction that makes one acceptable and the other not.
// It is also read as `u32` SLOTS rather than as bytes at offsets, so no endianness is
// assumed: the four header fields are slots 0..3 of a `*mut u32`, and the kernel pads every
// event's length to keep the next one aligned.
const WATCH_EV_HEADER: usize = 16

// Win32 `FILE_NOTIFY_CHANGE_FILE_NAME | _DIR_NAME | _LAST_WRITE`. Attributes and size are
// deliberately absent: a size change without a write is not a content change a build cares
// about, and both would fire more often for no more information.
const WATCH_WIN_FILTER: u32 = 19
const WATCH_WAIT_SIGNALED: i32 = 0          // WAIT_OBJECT_0
const WATCH_INVALID_HANDLE: i64 = 0 - 1     // INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE is (HANDLE)-1, not null

// POSIX `EINVAL` / Win32 `ERROR_INVALID_NAME` — the refusal this module reports before
// touching the platform, spelled as the platform's own number so `syserr.category`
// classifies it like any other failure. The same pair `std/sysfs` uses.
const WATCH_EINVAL: i32 = 22
const WATCH_ERROR_INVALID_NAME: i32 = 123

@cfg(posix) @no_alloc @no_os fn bad_path_code() -> i32 { return WATCH_EINVAL }
@cfg(windows) @no_alloc @no_os fn bad_path_code() -> i32 { return WATCH_ERROR_INVALID_NAME }

// ---- the handle ---------------------------------------------------------------
//
// One `Watcher` is one directory. Scalars and owned buffers only: a Jestyr borrow is
// second-class, so a handle cannot own borrowed storage.
//
// Three of the four platform fields are dead on each platform, and that is deliberate rather
// than wasteful. A `@cfg`-guarded STRUCT would mean two types that typeck keys on one name —
// the hazard §2.1e records for functions, with worse consequences for a layout — so the
// struct is unconditional and the OPERATIONS are guarded. Three unused words per watcher is
// a price worth paying to keep exactly one `Watcher` type in the program.
//
// **There is no `allowed` field**, and its absence is the point: an earlier draft carried one
// alongside `open`, written in three places and read in none. Two fields that must agree are
// two fields that can disagree — the same argument `std/syserr` makes for storing two fields
// and computing the third.
@move pub struct Watcher {
    fd: i64,            // POSIX: the inotify fd
    wd: i32,            // POSIX: the watch descriptor within that fd
    buf: *mut u32,      // POSIX: the kernel's read buffer, u32-slotted (see WATCH_EV_HEADER)
    cell: *mut cptr,    // Windows: a heap cell holding the HANDLE, so a Poller can reach it
    open: bool,
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn is_open(read w: Watcher) -> bool { return w.open }

// The value a failed open hands back, and the one a caller uses for a slot it has not
// filled. Exported because a module-qualified struct LITERAL does not parse, so a caller
// writing `catch` has no other way to name it — the same reason `sysnet.closed()` and
// `sysfs.missing()` exist.
//
// Every operation on it refuses, which is what makes ignoring the error survivable rather
// than a dereference of a handle that was never opened.
@no_alloc @no_os pub fn closed() -> Watcher {
    return Watcher{ fd: 0 - 1, wd: 0, buf: null, cell: null, open: false }
}

// The value to hand `runtime.watch`. **It must be paired with `poller(w)`** — the two come
// from the same module and mean nothing apart, which is the one invariant this module asks a
// caller to hold.
//
// On POSIX it is the inotify fd, which is why `syspoll.host()` services it unchanged. On
// Windows it is the HANDLE's own numeric value, which makes it unique per watcher without
// inventing an id space.
@cfg(posix) @no_alloc @no_os pub fn handle(read w: Watcher) -> i64 { return w.fd }

@cfg(windows) @no_os pub fn handle(read w: Watcher) -> i64 {
    if w.open == false { return 0 - 1 }
    var h: cptr = null
    unsafe { h = (w.cell + 0).* }
    return h as i64
}

// ---- opening ------------------------------------------------------------------

// Start watching `path`. The directory must exist; a watcher on a path that appears later
// is a policy (retry, or watch the parent) and belongs to the caller.
//
// `fs.Fs` is reused rather than a fourth capability type invented, for the reason
// `std/sysfs` gives: it is a POLICY value, and one handle threading through `std/fs`,
// `std/walk`, `sysfs` and here is worth more than tier purity. Refusal is reported by TYPE,
// so `fs.refused(f)` does not move.
@cfg(posix) pub fn open_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> Watcher !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(WatchRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(WatchFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    let fd: i64 = inotify_init1(WATCH_IN_NONBLOCK)
    if fd < 0 { return err(WatchFailed(syserr.last_raw())) }

    // The errno is read BEFORE the `CString` is freed: `free` is not required to preserve
    // it on every libc, so anything between the failing call and the read is a chance to
    // lose the reason. The same discipline every operation in `std/sysfs` follows.
    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let wd: i32 = inotify_add_watch(fd, cstring.as_cstr(c), WATCH_IN_MASK)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if wd < 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if wd < 0 {
        close(fd)
        return err(WatchFailed(code))
    }

    var buf: *mut u32 = alloc(u32, WATCH_BUF_CAP / 4)
    return ok(Watcher{ fd: fd, wd: wd, buf: buf, cell: null, open: true })
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn open_dir(mut f: fs.Fs, read a: Allocator, read path: str) -> Watcher !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if fs.can_read(f) == false { return err(WatchRefused) }
    if cstring.has_interior_nul(path) { return err(WatchFailed(bad_path_code())) }

    var c: CString = cstring.from_str(a, path)
    let h: cptr = FindFirstChangeNotificationA(cstring.as_cstr(c), 0, WATCH_WIN_FILTER)
    // **The failure test is the HANDLE, not the error code.** Inferring failure from a
    // non-zero `GetLastError` would conflate two different questions, and it fails in the
    // direction that matters: a failed call that left the code at zero would come back as a
    // successfully opened watcher wrapping `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`. `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` is
    // `(HANDLE)-1` and not null, which is why both are tested.
    let bad: bool = h == null or (h as i64) == WATCH_INVALID_HANDLE
    var code: i32 = 0
    if bad { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    cstring.free(c)
    if bad { return err(WatchFailed(code)) }

    // The handle goes on the heap rather than only in the struct, because a `Poller` reaches
    // its state through a `*mut u8` and a Jestyr borrow cannot outlive the call that made
    // it. The cell IS the poller's context, and `close_watch` frees it.
    var cell: *mut cptr = alloc(cptr, 1)
    unsafe { (cell + 0).* = h }
    return ok(Watcher{ fd: 0 - 1, wd: 0, buf: null, cell: cell, open: true })
}

// Stop watching and release the platform's handle. Idempotent on the Jestyr value.
//
// Answers nothing — a failed close tells a caller nothing actionable, since the handle is
// gone either way, which is why the failure is REPORTED rather than returned as a status.
// That signature is only spellable because a fallible function may now have no return type.
//
// **Named `close_watch` rather than `close`**: this module binds libc's `close` as an
// extern, and an extern's name is a C symbol reserved within its own module. See the
// bindings above.
@cfg(posix) pub fn close_watch(mut w: Watcher) !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if w.open == false { return }
    inotify_rm_watch(w.fd, w.wd)
    let rc: i32 = close(w.fd)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc != 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    free_ptr(w.buf)
    w.buf = null
    w.open = false
    if rc != 0 { return err(WatchFailed(code)) }
}

@cfg(windows) pub fn close_watch(mut w: Watcher) !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if w.open == false { return }
    var h: cptr = null
    unsafe { h = (w.cell + 0).* }
    let rc: i32 = FindCloseChangeNotification(h)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if rc == 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    free_ptr(w.cell)
    w.cell = null
    w.open = false
    if rc == 0 { return err(WatchFailed(code)) }
}

// ---- the poller ---------------------------------------------------------------

// On POSIX an inotify fd is a file descriptor and `poll(2)` waits on it exactly as it waits
// on a socket, so this is `std/syspoll`'s poller unchanged — **the integration cost of file
// watching on Linux is one line**, which is the property `runtime.Poller` being a fn pointer
// was supposed to buy and here is the receipt.
@cfg(posix) pub fn poller(read w: Watcher) -> runtime.Poller {
    return syspoll.host()
}

// On Windows it is not a file descriptor and `WSAPoll` cannot see it, so the watcher brings
// its own poller. See the header for the limitation this creates and for why the fix lives
// in `std/sysnet` rather than here.
//
// It waits on ITS OWN handle for the whole timeout and marks ready only the entry whose
// handle matches. Waiting even when its handle is not registered is deliberate: reporting
// "nothing ready" instantly would turn a runtime holding a foreign handle into a busy loop,
// where waiting makes it idle correctly and merely never fire. Never falsely ready, never
// spinning — the two properties a poller that cannot see everything can still keep.
@cfg(windows) fn win_wait(ctx: *mut u8, fds: *mut i64, ready: *mut u8, n: usize, timeout_nanos: i64) -> i64 {
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < n {
        unsafe { (ready + i).* = 0 as u8 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if n == 0 { return 0 }

    // **A closed watcher's poller reports nothing rather than dereferencing null.** This is
    // reachable without doing anything unusual: `open_dir(...) catch closed()` is the
    // documented fallback, and a caller who installs its poller before checking `is_open`
    // would otherwise crash inside the loop instead of at the failed open. Nothing-ready is
    // the honest answer — a watcher that was never opened will never become readable.
    if ctx == null { return 0 }

    var ms: i64 = 0
    if timeout_nanos > 0 { ms = (timeout_nanos + 999999) / 1000000 }
    if ms > 2147483647 { ms = 2147483647 }

    var cell: *mut cptr = ctx as *mut cptr
    var h: cptr = null
    unsafe { h = (cell + 0).* }
    if h == null { return 0 }
    let rc: i32 = WaitForSingleObject(h, ms as u32)
    if rc != WATCH_WAIT_SIGNALED { return 0 }

    // Signaled. The handle stays signaled until `drain` re-arms it, so nothing is lost by
    // reporting it against every matching registration.
    let me: i64 = h as i64
    var count: i64 = 0
    var k: usize = 0
    for k < n {
        var fd: i64 = 0
        unsafe { fd = (fds + k).* }
        if fd == me {
            unsafe { (ready + k).* = 1 as u8 }
            count = count + 1
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    return count
}

@cfg(windows) @no_os pub fn poller(read w: Watcher) -> runtime.Poller {
    return runtime.poller(w.cell as *mut u8, &win_wait)
}

// ---- draining -----------------------------------------------------------------

// Report what has changed since the last call, into caller storage. Returns how many
// changes were written into `evs`; their names are packed into `names`.
//
// **Never blocks on either platform**, so calling it when nothing has happened is free and
// answers 0. See `WATCH_IN_NONBLOCK` for why that contract was chosen over readiness-first.
//
// **DRAIN UNTIL IT ANSWERS 0.** One call is not one file operation and never was: writing a
// new file is `IN_CREATE` and `IN_CLOSE_WRITE` on Linux, and on Windows the notification
// handle re-arms straight back into a signaled state because the directory genuinely
// changed more than once. The two platforms coalesce differently — Linux returns both
// events from a single read, Windows needs a second call — so a caller that drains once and
// stops has written code that works on one of them. What both promise is CONVERGENCE: with
// nothing new happening this reaches 0 in a bounded number of calls, and only then does the
// watch stop waking the loop. `a_real_change_wakes_the_loop_and_drains` asserts exactly
// that pair, and it asserts them separately, so a `drain` that answered 0 without consuming
// anything would still be caught.
//
// **A full buffer becomes a `WATCH_OVERFLOW`, not a silent truncation.** If `evs` or `names`
// cannot hold everything the platform reported, the last slot written is an overflow marker
// and the remainder is discarded — because the data has already left the kernel and cannot
// be put back. That deliberately produces the same signal as the kernel's own queue
// overflow, so a caller has exactly one recovery path and it is reachable by giving this
// function a small buffer rather than by putting a kernel under load — which is what
// `lost_events_are_reported_rather_than_silently_dropped` does, with the anti-vacuity
// control (the same events into a buffer that FITS report all of them and no marker) beside
// it.
@cfg(posix) pub fn drain(mut w: Watcher, mut evs: []Change, mut names: []u8) -> usize !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if w.open == false { return err(WatchRefused) }
    if evs.len == 0 { return ok(0) }

    // `struct iovec` — two pointer-sized words, built as `usize` slots so there is no offset
    // to get wrong and no byte order to assume. See the header.
    var iov: *mut usize = alloc(usize, 2)
    unsafe { (iov + 0).* = (w.buf as i64) as usize }
    unsafe { (iov + 1).* = WATCH_BUF_CAP }
    let got: i64 = readv(w.fd, iov as cptr, 1)
    var code: i32 = 0
    if got < 0 { code = syserr.last_raw() }
    free_ptr(iov)

    // Nothing pending. `EAGAIN`/`EWOULDBLOCK` on a non-blocking fd is the ANSWER "no events",
    // not a failure, and reporting it as one would make an idle loop error every pass.
    if got < 0 {
        if code == 11 or code == 35 { return ok(0) }
        return err(WatchFailed(code))
    }
    return ok(parse_inotify(w.buf, got as usize, evs, names))
}

// Walk a buffer of inotify events and turn it into `Change`s. Returns how many were written.
//
// **Public, and NOT `@cfg`-guarded, which is the most important decision in this module.**
// This is where a file watcher is actually wrong — a transposed mask bit or an off-by-one in
// the name padding yields plausible events forever — and guarding it would mean the parser
// only ever runs on the platform whose CI job is the one nobody is looking at. It needs no
// operating system: it is a pure function of a byte count and a buffer, exactly as
// `syserr.category` is a pure function of a code and a numbering system, and for the same
// payoff — **the part most likely to be wrong is the part under test on every host.**
//
// **`avail` is the byte count a read RETURNED, and the caller owes that it does not exceed
// what `buf` actually holds** — this takes a raw pointer, so there is no length to check
// against, which is the ordinary bargain in this tier. Everything downstream of it is
// bounded: a name that would run past `avail` and a slot index past `evs.len` both stop with
// an overflow marker rather than reading on.
//
// `buf` is `*mut u32`
// rather than `*mut u8` so the four header fields are SLOTS instead of offsets, which is
// what removes the byte-order assumption; the kernel's own padding keeps every event
// 4-aligned, and an unaligned one is refused rather than guessed at.
pub fn parse_inotify(buf: *mut u32, avail: usize, mut evs: []Change, mut names: []u8) -> usize {
    var n: usize = 0
    var off: usize = 0
    var noff: usize = 0
    for off + WATCH_EV_HEADER <= avail {
        // Every event is 4-aligned by the kernel's own padding. A buffer that is not says
        // the ABI assumption above is wrong, and stopping with an overflow marker is the
        // honest answer — a misparse would invent names out of the middle of a struct.
        if (off & 3) != 0 { return stamp_overflow(evs, n) }
        let slot: usize = off / 4
        var mask: u32 = 0
        var nlen: u32 = 0
        unsafe { mask = (buf + (slot + 1)).* }
        unsafe { nlen = (buf + (slot + 3)).* }
        let namelen: usize = nlen as usize
        if off + WATCH_EV_HEADER + namelen > avail { return stamp_overflow(evs, n) }

        // The kernel dropped events. Everything after this point in the stream is still
        // valid, but the caller has already missed something, so the marker is emitted and
        // the rest is abandoned — reporting the survivors would understate the damage.
        if (mask & WATCH_IN_Q_OVERFLOW) != 0 { return stamp_overflow(evs, n) }

        let kind: i32 = kind_of(mask)
        if kind != 0 {
            if n >= evs.len { return stamp_overflow(evs, n) }
            // The name is NUL-PADDED to the alignment, not NUL-terminated at its length, so
            // the real length is up to the first NUL rather than `nlen`.
            let real: usize = name_len_at(buf, off + WATCH_EV_HEADER, namelen)
            if noff + real > names.len { return stamp_overflow(evs, n) }
            copy_name(buf, off + WATCH_EV_HEADER, real, names, noff)
            evs[n] = Change{ kind: kind, name_off: noff, name_len: real }
            noff = noff + real
            n = n + 1
        }
        off = off + WATCH_EV_HEADER + namelen
    }
    return n
}

// Replace the last usable slot with an overflow marker. Returns the new count.
//
// When `evs` is completely full the LAST change is overwritten rather than the marker being
// dropped: losing one reported change is strictly better than losing the fact that changes
// were lost.
fn stamp_overflow(mut evs: []Change, n: usize) -> usize {
    if evs.len == 0 { return 0 }
    var at: usize = n
    if at >= evs.len { at = evs.len - 1 }
    evs[at] = Change{ kind: WATCH_OVERFLOW, name_off: 0, name_len: 0 }
    return at + 1
}

// Which of this module's kinds an inotify mask is, or 0 for one it did not ask for.
//
// The order matters where a mask carries more than one bit: a file that is created and
// written before the queue is drained arrives with `IN_CREATE | IN_CLOSE_WRITE`, and
// reporting it as CREATED is the more useful of the two — a caller that reacts to creation
// will read the file anyway.
@no_alloc @no_os fn kind_of(mask: u32) -> i32 {
    if (mask & WATCH_IN_CREATE) != 0 { return WATCH_CREATED }
    if (mask & WATCH_IN_DELETE) != 0 { return WATCH_DELETED }
    if (mask & WATCH_IN_MOVED_TO) != 0 { return WATCH_MOVED_IN }
    if (mask & WATCH_IN_MOVED_FROM) != 0 { return WATCH_MOVED_OUT }
    if (mask & WATCH_IN_CLOSE_WRITE) != 0 { return WATCH_WRITTEN }
    return 0
}

// The name's real length: up to `cap` bytes at byte offset `at`, stopping at the first NUL.
fn name_len_at(buf: *mut u32, at: usize, cap: usize) -> usize {
    var p: *mut u8 = buf as *mut u8
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < cap {
        var b: u8 = 0
        unsafe { b = (p + (at + i)).* }
        if (b as i32) == 0 { break }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return i
}

fn copy_name(buf: *mut u32, at: usize, len: usize, mut names: []u8, to: usize) {
    var p: *mut u8 = buf as *mut u8
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < len {
        var b: u8 = 0
        unsafe { b = (p + (at + i)).* }
        names[to + i] = b
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// Windows reports THAT the directory changed, so a drain is at most one change and it never
// carries a name. Re-arming is what makes the handle go quiet again, and it happens HERE
// rather than in the poller because the poller may run without the caller consuming
// anything — a watch is level-triggered, and re-arming from the wait would drop a change the
// caller never saw.
@cfg(windows) pub fn drain(mut w: Watcher, mut evs: []Change, mut names: []u8) -> usize !{ WatchFailed(i32), WatchRefused } {
    if w.open == false { return err(WatchRefused) }
    if evs.len == 0 { return ok(0) }
    var h: cptr = null
    unsafe { h = (w.cell + 0).* }
    if WaitForSingleObject(h, 0) != WATCH_WAIT_SIGNALED { return ok(0) }
    evs[0] = Change{ kind: WATCH_RESCAN, name_off: 0, name_len: 0 }
    if FindNextChangeNotification(h) == 0 { return err(WatchFailed(syserr.last_raw())) }
    return ok(1)
}
